  "query_preview": "..\nKey Message 1: Possible record highs Wednesday.\n\nLook for 5h heights to build head into midweek with westerly\nflow aiding in heating up the lower levels. Highs Wednesday are\nlikely to reach close to or even exceed records. See climate\nsection below.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Front brings mainly mountain showers/storms\nThursday followed by a stronger front Friday with airmass change\nfor the weekend.\n\nUpper ridge starts to flatten ahead of a front Thursday. Models\nare in agreement that the mountains will be having a better\nprobability for having measurable showers/few storms compared to\nthe Piedmont.\n\nA few storms Thursday afternoon could be strong mainly north of\nI-64. Daytime heating combined with boundary- layer dewpoints \nin the mid 60s to low 70s F is expected to contribute to pockets\nof moderate instability with MLCAPE approaching 1000-1250 J/kg \nby mid-late afternoon ahead of the cold front. \n\nThe main front tracks across Friday and will bring shower\nchances over the mountains but should be fairly dry with limited\nmoisture east of the mountains. West to northwest winds should\nbe gusty behind the front as temps drop from the 70s/80s Friday\nto the 40s and 50s Friday evening.\n\nThere could be as high as a 20 degree temperature difference \nbetween Friday and Saturday as Saturday afternoon highs may only\nget into the lower 50s to lower 60s. Sunday morning, so far, \nlooks to be the coldest with lows in the upper 20s to lower 30s\nin the mountains. This could be the first freeze of the season \nfor the mountains with frost in the Piedmont.",
